selection, output selected_cluster_ids in sorted order (presumably ascending). We'll output sorted ascending.\n\nThe algorithm:\n1. Build a mapping from cluster_id to cluster data.\n2. For each cluster, compute stability: sum over point_exit_lambdas of max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda). Use birth_lambda from the cluster object.\n3. Filter clusters where size >= min_cluster_size. These are the \"retained\" clusters.\n4. For each retained cluster, create an object with cluster_id, size, stability.\n5. For selection, we need to consider the hierarchical relationships. Use the parent dict to build a tree (only retained clusters? But blocking ancestors must consider all ancestors, even those not retained? In HDBSCAN, we select from the condensed tree, which includes only clusters that survived the min cluster size filter. The parents are among those clusters? Actually in condensed tree, each cluster has a parent (or None). The clusters that are too small are not included. So we only consider retained clusters for selection. The parent map might reference clusters that are not retained (if they were too small? But in typical HDBSCAN, the condensed tree includes only clusters that meet min size. However here the input includes all clusters, some with size < min_cluster_size. The parent map references those cluster ids. We need to decide: Do we include only retained clusters in the selection tree? The algorithm says \"Drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size.\" Then \"For each retained cluster sum ...\". Then selection. So the selection is only among retained clusters. But the parent relationships among retained clusters might be indirect if intermediate clusters were dropped. We need to build a tree of retained clusters: if a retained cluster's parent is also retained, then it's direct parent; if parent is not retained, we need to find the nearest retained ancestor (like in HDBSCAN, the condensed tree already has that property because clusters that are too small are not present). In standard HDBSCAN, the condensed tree is built from the raw hierarchy by removing clusters below min size, and then the parent of a cluster is the smallest enclosing cluster that also meets min size. However here the input provides parent map directly from the raw hierarchy? The fixture shows parent: c0->null, c1->c0, c2->c0. All clusters have size: c0=7, c1=4, c2=2. min_cluster_size=3. So c2 is dropped. c0 and c1 are retained. parent of c1 is c0, which is retained, so okay. But if parent was c2, which is dropped, we need to find ancestor. Likely the input parent map is intended to be the condensed tree parent? The task says \"Condense a supplied hierarchy under a minimum cluster size\" meaning we need to condense it ourselves. So we should not trust that the parent map already reflects the condensation. Instead, we must construct the condensed tree by dropping small clusters and recomputing parent relationships.\n\nLet's derive: The input gives a hierarchy (tree) of clusters with parent references, possibly including clusters below min size. We need to compute the condensed tree: keep only clusters with size >= min_cluster_size. Then for each retained cluster, its parent in the condensed tree is the nearest ancestor (in the original hierarchy) that is also retained (or None if none). This is standard HDBSCAN condensation.\n\nThen compute stability for each retained cluster as described.\n\nThen selection: rank retained clusters by stability descending, then by cluster_id descending (to break ties). Process in that order. For each cluster, if no descendant (in the condensed tree) has been selected already, then select it, and mark all its ancestors (in condensed tree) as blocked (i.e., cannot be selected later). Finally, output selected_cluster_ids sorted ascending.\n\nNote: \"block its ancestors\" means that after selecting a cluster, any ancestor cannot be selected. This is typical.\n\nAlso note: The selection algorithm often uses clustering tree where descendants are children. But we need to consider the tree structure. Since we only have parent pointers, we can build children lists for retained clusters.\n\nImplementation steps in solve(case):\n- Extract case['min_cluster_size'], case['clusters'], case['parent'].\n- Build dict clusters_data: cluster_id -> {size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas}\n- Filter clusters where size >= min_cluster_size -> retained_ids set.\n- Build mapping from cluster_id to original parent_id (from case['parent']).\n- Build condensed parent: for each retained cluster, traverse up the original parent chain until we find a retained cluster or None. That becomes its condensed parent. Also note: if a retained cluster has no retained ancestor, condensed parent is None.\n- Build children list for condensed tree: for each retained cluster with condensed parent p, add cluster_id to children[p] (p may be None? For root, we don't need children of None but we can handle).\n- Compute stability for each retained cluster: sum(max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) for exit_lambda in point_exit_lambdas). Use birth_lambda from the cluster data.\n- Create list of retained cluster info: {cluster_id, size, stability}.\n- Sort this list by stability descending, then cluster_id descending (ties).\n- Initialize selected = set(), blocked_ancestors = set()? Actually blocking ancestors: we need to prevent selection of any ancestor of a selected cluster. We can maintain a set of ancestors that are forbidden. But simpler: process sorted list, for each cluster, check if any descendant (in condensed tree) is already selected. If not, select it and then mark all its ancestors (up to root) as blocked (or just add to a set of blocked clusters). Since we process in order, ancestors that are blocked will be skipped. But we need to also avoid selecting a cluster that has a selected descendant? That's the condition: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it\". So we need to check if any