re below. So c's ancestors are the ones that have c as descendant. So we add each ancestor a (not including c) to blocked set. Then later when we encounter a, it will be blocked. Note: what if we select a cluster, and later encounter a descendant? That descendant would be processed later? Since we sort by stability descending, if a descendant has higher stability than ancestor, it would be processed first and block the ancestor. If a descendant has lower stability, it would be processed later. In that case, when we process the descendant, we need to check if any of its ancestors are already selected? Actually the condition is only about descendants blocking ancestors. If an ancestor is already selected, does that block the descendant? According to spec: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it\". It does not say that an already selected ancestor blocks the descendant. So a descendant can be selected even if an ancestor is selected. In HDBSCAN's condensed tree selection, once a cluster is selected, all its ancestors are no longer considered (because they are \"blocked\"), but descendants are still free to be selected? Actually standard HDBSCAN: clusters are selected from leaves upward; if a cluster is selected, its ancestors are not selected. But descendants are not selected if they are within the selected cluster? I think the typical algorithm: after ranking, process from highest stability to lowest. For each cluster, if it has not been selected and none of its descendants have been selected, then select it and mark all ancestors as not selectable (because they would contain selected points). This means that once a cluster is selected, its ancestors are blocked, but its descendants might still be selected if they have higher stability? Wait, if a descendant is selected, that blocks the ancestor. But if an ancestor is selected first (because it has higher stability), then later we check a descendant: does it have a selected descendant? No