se, you keep the descendants. But the task says \"Rank clusters by decreasing stability and cluster ID. Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" This seems to be a different algorithm: it explicitly says \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" meaning that if a descendant is already selected, then this cluster cannot be selected. It does not mention ancestors blocking descendants. So if an ancestor is selected first, descendants are still free to be selected. But then \"block its ancestors\" after selection means that if we select a descendant later, its ancestors become blocked, but they may already be selected? That would be contradictory. I think the intended algorithm is: process in descending stability; for each cluster, if it is not already blocked (by a selected descendant), select it and block all its ancestors. This ensures that an ancestor can only be selected if none of its descendants are selected. And a descendant can be selected even if an ancestor is already selected? But if an ancestor is selected first, then when we later process a descendant, the descendant will check if any of its descendants are selected (none) and will pass. But the ancestor is already selected, so we would have both selected, which is wrong. However, note that when the ancestor was selected, it blocked its ancestors (not descendants). So the descendant is not blocked. So to avoid double selection, we need to also block the descendants of a selected cluster? But the spec only mentions blocking ancestors. Perhaps \"block its ancestors\" is meant to imply that the cluster itself is selected, and all ancestors become ineligible. The clause \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it\" implies that if a descendant is already selected, the ancestor is blocked. It doesn't say that an already selected ancestor blocks the descendant. So the algorithm might allow selecting both an ancestor and a descen