eaves to root, but the spec says ranking by decreasing stability. Another approach: after selecting a cluster, block its descendants as well. But spec says \"block its ancestors\" only. However, in the standard HDBSCAN paper, the selection algorithm: sort clusters by stability, then process from highest to lowest. For each cluster, if it is not already marked as \"selected\" (i.e., part of a selected cluster), then mark it and its subtree as selected (or mark ancestors as not selectable). Actually I'm recalling: In the HDBSCAN implementation, when you select a cluster, you mark the entire path to the root as \"selected\", and then all clusters in that path are considered \"selected\" and cannot be selected again. But the selection is done by comparing stability of a cluster to the sum of stabilities of its children. The algorithm used in scikit-learn's HDBSCAN is: For each node in the condensed tree, compute the stability and the sum of stabilities of its descendants (the cluster's \"self-stability\" vs \"inherited stability\"). The selection is done by recursively from leaves: if the cluster's own stability > sum of children stabilities, then the cluster is selected and the children are not; otherwise, the children are selected and the cluster's stability is set to the sum. This is different from the rank-and-block algorithm.\n\nGiven the spec explicitly says \"Rank clusters by decreasing stability and cluster ID. Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" This matches a specific algorithm: sort by stability descending, then for each cluster in that order, if none of its descendants have been selected yet, select it and mark all its ancestors as blocked (so they cannot be selected later). This ensures that selected clusters are not descendants of each other (since if a descendant is selected, it blocks the ancestor). But it does not prevent an ancestor from being selected after a descendant? Actually if we process in descending stability, a descendant with lower stability will come later after an ancestor with higher stability. If the ancestor is selected first, then later when we process the descendant, we check if any of its descendants are selected; none are, so it would get selected, causing overlap. So to prevent this, we must ensure that we process clusters from leaves to root, i.e., descendants before ancestors. But the ranking by decreasing stability may not guarantee that; stability can be higher for ancestors due to larger size. So the algorithm would fail.\n\nWait, could it be that \"no already selected descendant\" includes the case where an ancestor is already selected? No, descendant means lower in the tree. So ancestor is not a descendant. So the algorithm as described allows overlapping clusters if the ancestor is selected first. Possibly the intended algorithm is that the ranking is by decreasing stability, but within that, we process in such a way that we only consider clusters that are not already blocked by a selected descendant. And \"block its ancestors\" means that after selecting a cluster, we mark all ancestors as having a selected descendant. Then later when we process those ancestors (which have lower stability), they will see that they have a selected descendant (i.e., they themselves are blocked? Actually the condition checks \"no already selected descendant blocks it\". For an ancestor, the selected descendant (the one we just selected) is a descendant, so the ancestor is blocked. But if the ancestor has higher stability, it would have been processed earlier, before the descendant was selected. So it wouldn't have seen that descendant. So the algorithm would not work with simple sorting. Therefore, the correct order must be such that descendants are processed before ancestors. That can be achieved by sorting by depth descending (leaves first), or by sorting by stability and then processing in that order but also ensuring that when a cluster is