t a descendant of c1 (c1 is descendant of c0), so c1 has no selected descendant (c0 is ancestor). So c1 would also be selected. That would result in both clusters selected, which is incorrect because they overlap. However, in HDBSCAN, if the parent cluster has higher stability, it is selected instead of the child. The algorithm described would fail. So maybe the spec intends that after selecting a cluster, you also block all its descendants? But it says \"block its ancestors\", not descendants. Or maybe the condition should be \"no already selected cluster is a descendant or ancestor\"? That would make sense: select a cluster only if no selected cluster is in its subtree (descendant) and no selected cluster is an ancestor. That's the typical mutual exclusivity. But the spec explicitly says \"only when no already selected descendant blocks it\". That is one directional. Possibly the interpretation is that you only check descendants, but by processing in descending stability and blocking ancestors after selection, you ensure that when a cluster with higher stability is selected, its ancestors are blocked, but its descendants remain eligible. However, if a descendant has higher stability, it would be processed first and block the ancestor, so they don't get both. If an ancestor has higher stability, it would be processed first; then later when a descendant is processed, the descendant will find that it has a selected ancestor (not descendant), so it will not be blocked and will be selected. That gives overlap. So to avoid that, we must ensure that descendants are always processed before ancestors. In the tree, if the current order (by stability) does not guarantee that, we might need to enforce a topological order: process from leaves upward. But the spec says \"Rank clusters by decreasing stability and cluster ID.\" So we must follow that order, even if it leads to overlapping? Possibly the intended algorithm is that we select only clusters that are \"stable\" and then we output them, and if both are selected, that's okay because they are different clusters? But they are nested, and HDBSCAN selects disjoint clusters. The task description says \"condensed tree stability ... select stable clusters\". The typical output is a flat clustering where each point is assigned to exactly one selected cluster. So overlapping clusters shouldn't be selected. So the algorithm must prevent that.\n\nLet's look at the spec more carefully: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" Could it be that \"descendant\" is meant to be \"ancestor\"? Or that the blocking includes both directions? Another possibility: The phrase \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" means we check if any descendant is already selected; if so, block this cluster (i.e., don't select it). Then after selecting, block its ancestors (so they cannot be selected). This ensures that once a cluster is selected, all its ancestors are blocked. But to prevent an ancestor from being selected after a descendant, we need to process in order that descendants come first. Since we sort by stability descending, if descendant has higher stability, it comes first and blocks ancestor. If ancestor has higher stability, it comes first, but then later descendant is processed: its descendants are none, so it doesn't see any selected descendant; it will be selected. That gives two selected clusters. To fix, we could also require that a cluster is not selected if any of its ancestors are already selected. But that's not in the spec.\n\nMaybe the algorithm is: process clusters in decreasing stability; for each cluster, if it is not already blocked (by any selected descendant of it), then select it and block all its ancestors. At then end, the selected clusters are exactly those that we selected. But we need to ensure that when we block ancestors, we also block the cluster itself? No. In the example where ancestor has higher stability, after 